back for blood the co-op zombie survival  game and spiritual successor to left 4  dead is now available for everyone i  don't think it's a terribly complicated  game and it's designed to get you and  some buddies shooting as quickly as  possible that said in my time with the  game i have discovered a few tricks and  other things of note that will improve  your experience in the fight against the  written  i really wish they just called them  zombies  [Music]  but let's start with a bit of a warning  as its name suggests back for blood is  all about that sweet sweet 4 player  co-op but does the game offer solo play  yes  but you might not want to play it solo  campaign as it's called is a separate  option in the menu that limits almost  all forms of progression that means no  achievements no stat tracking and no  supply points the in-game currency used  to unlock new cards and cosmetics the  only thing you can unlock in solo are  new acts and levels as you play through  but without earning the achievements and  supply points you would have gotten  playing online it's gotta feel bad and  no you can't play the online mode and  set everyone to bots it forces you to  match make developer turtle rock studios  has already made a statement about this  on twitter saying that it is discussing  ways to address the lack of progression  in solo mode but with no time frame for  when said changes might occur your best  bet right now is to just avoid solo mode  altogether  back for blood's card and deck manager  is the game's big feature to help it  stand out from left 4 dead letting  players essentially create custom  classes it's still a bit too early for  me to have some amazing game breaking  bills to recommend to you although i'm  sure you can find some on the internet  but i do have some advice to help you  get started now i know i just said that  solo is kind of worthless but that's not  completely true as you can use it as a  testing ground for dex solo has his own  separate deck manager with access to all  of the cards from the get-go meaning you  can spend the time building the ideal  deck or testing out certain cards in  solo and see if you like them then once  you earn those cards you can recreate  that deck online  that said deck building systems and  games can be real hit or miss for  players either it is extremely yours and  you're eager to put in the time  or you don't care and just want to play  the game so if you're the latter first  off i wouldn't stress it too much having  an amazing deck build isn't essential to  success certainly not the beginning of  the game and not on recruit difficulty  just play the game using one of the  default decks you'll earn cards slowly  but steadily from supply lines giving  you the space to see if you like the  card you just earned and not  overwhelming you with a massive list of  them as you play the game you'll figure  out what playstyle you like and then you  can start building a deck around that  if you find yourself enjoying melee  weapons give yourself some melee cards  really is that straightforward and if  you really just have no interest at all  hey you can always look up deck bills on  the internet  last tip i have around decks is that the  order of your cards actually matters so  place the cards with perks you want  right up at the top a great pick to  start with would be a fortune card like  copper scavenger which increases copper  pile spawns leading to a larger boost in  the long run  let's talk about melee no not melee  weapons but the dinky little bash attack  everyone has where you sucker punch a  zombie the thing is it's not very  effective at killing but rather pushing  a ridden away from you to give you some  breathing room but wouldn't you rather  just kill him that's why regardless of  what deck you're building i recommend  you include the card combat knife which  turns that pitiful bash into a full on  knife stab capable of killing basic  ridden with one thrust now that you'll  actually be using your knife bash more  often here's a pro tip it doesn't  interrupt your reload so next time  you're swarmed by zombies and need to  reload remember to melee and get in a  few extra kills  one of the most useful things you can  come across in a run is a locked door  these special rooms typically contain  several piles of copper good weapon  drops hell stations etc they're always  worth opening but you do need a tool kit  you can get lucky and find them in the  world but your safest option is to buy  them from the shop in the safe room  coordinate with your team and make sure  you always have at least one tool kit on  hand typically there is only one lock  door in each level but it is possible  for there to be multiple so that is  worth considering  chances are you aren't always going to  be playing back for blood with a group  of friends and will be relying on randos  through the matchmaking it's also pretty  likely they won't be using voice chat at  least not intentionally and let's be  honest you probably don't want to use it  yourself this is where the game's  excellent ping system becomes your best  friend pointing out special ridden ammo  weapons or whatever is as simple as  pressing a button cue on pc and left  bumper on consoles remember you are a  team so help your buddies out and ping  anything you're not interested in taking  yourself  you might think you have to play all the  way through an act in one go but that is  not the case after the end of every  level you can leave the game and return  to fort hope and your progress will be  saved for you to return to later maybe  you just want to take a break or spend  some supply points to unlock new cars  and cosmetics speaking of if you do end  up modifying your deck and then return  to an in-progress act those changes will  not carry over you are stuck with the  deck you started the run with and you  can't swap characters part way through  cosmetic changes like outfits and weapon  